   - Location: Name extraction creating incomplete \"De\" + descriptor resolution for \"old man\"\n    92→    - Fix: Improve compound name handling for \"De Lacey\", \"Van Helsing\" style names. Merge descriptive references.\n    93→    - Impact: -0.1 points\n    94→\n    95→11. **Inconsistent canonical names using relational descriptors**\n    96→    - Problem: \"my father\" and \"my mother\" used as canonical names instead of \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" and \"Caroline Frankenstein\"\n    97→    - Evidence: Character profiles show \"my father\" (53 mentions) and \"my mother\" (10 mentions) as main headings rather than proper names\n    98→    - Location: Canonical name selection in consensus.py\n    99→    - Fix: Prefer proper names over relational descriptors when selecting canonical name. The proper name should be canonical, with relational terms as aliases.\n   100→    - Impact: Usability issue (scoring included in Critical #1 and High #7)\n   101→\n   102→### LOW\n   103→None at this time.\n   104→\n   105→## Fix History\n   106→### Attempt 1: Fixed CLI output path handling\n   107→**Issue:** CLI ignored explicit `--html` and `--output` flags, creating timestamped directory instead\n   108→\n   109→**Fix Applied:**\n   110→- Modified `src/cli.py` lines 367-368: Added `output_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` for JSON output\n   111→- Modified `src/cli.py` lines 397-398: Added `html_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` for HTML output\n   112→\n   113→**Testing:** All 444 tests pass.\n   114→\n   115→**Result:** PASSED - CLI now respects explicit output paths\n   116→\n   117→### Attempt 2: Enhanced alias candidate pair generation for relational descriptors\n   118→**Issue:** Critical #1 from Attempt 1 - Relational/familial descriptors like \"my father\", \"Father\" not being paired with proper names like \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" for alias resolution\n   119→\n   120→**Root Cause:** The `_candidate_pairs_for_merge` function in `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` only generated candidate pairs based on:\n   121→1. Token overlap (shared words)\n   122→2. Substring matching\n   123→3. Known nickname variants\n   124→\n   125→Relational descriptors like \"my father\" share no tokens with \"Alphonse Frankenstein\", so they were never considered as candidates for the LLM pairwise merge decision.\n   126→\n   127→**Fix Applied:**\n   128→- Modified `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` lines 934-987\n   129→- Added new section in `_candidate_pairs_for_merge` to identify relational/familial descriptors (father, mother, uncle, etc.) and descriptive patterns (old man, creature, stranger, etc.)\n   130→- Pairs each relational/descriptive name with top 30 proper names for LLM evaluation\n   131→- The LLM's pairwise merge logic decides if they actually refer to the same person based on context\n   132→\n   133→**Testing:** All 444 tests pass.\n   134→\n   135→**Result:** PARTIAL SUCCESS\n   136→- ✅ \"the creature\" successfully merged with \"the monster\" and \"the wretch\" (aliases: \"the monster, the wretch\")\n   137→- ❌ \"my father\" (53), \"Father\" (6), \"M.\" (23), \"M. Frankenstein\" (2), \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (1) still separate\n   138→- ❌ \"Walton\" (6) and \"Captain Walton\" (1) still separate\n   139→- ❌ \"Clerval\" (55) and \"M. Clerval\" (2) still separate\n   140→\n   141→**Analysis:** The candidate pair generation is now working (proven by \"the creature\" merging correctly), but the LLM pairwise merge decision is rejecting merges for \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" OR the validation step is blocking them.\n   142→\n   143→### Attempt 3: Fixed validation logic to accept relational descriptor merges\n   144→**Issue:** Critical #1 from Attempt 2 - The `_validate_merge` function was rejecting all merges with no shared words, including valid relational descriptor merges like \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"\n   145→\n   146→**Root Cause Investigation:**\n   147→- Traced through `_validate_merge` function in `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py`\n   148→- Found that after checking various special cases, line 2001-2011 had a blanket rejection for ANY pair with zero shared words\n   149→- \"my father\" (words: \"my\", \"father\") and \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (words: \"alphonse\", \"frankenstein\") share NO words\n   150→- Since both are multi-word names, they didn't match the single-word special case logic\n   151→- The code reached the final \"no shared words = reject\" clause and was blocked\n   152→\n   153→**Fix Applied:**\n   154→- Modified `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` lines 2001-2042\n   155→- Added special case handling BEFORE the blanket rejection for relational/descriptive terms\n   156→- Detects if either name contains relational terms (father, mother, uncle, etc.) or descriptive patterns (creature, monster, man, etc.)\n   157→- For relational/descriptive pairs with chapter overlap, allow merge with confidence based on overlap ratio:\n   158→  - overlap_ratio > 0.3: confidence 0.75\n   159→  - any overlap: confidence 0.65\n   160→  - no overlap: reject with confidence 0.3\n   161→- This allows \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" to merge if they appear in overlapping chapters\n   162→\n   163→**Testing:** All 444 tests pass.\n   164→\n   165→**Expected Impact:** Should significantly improve character alias resolution for Frankenstein, reducing character fragmentation.\n   166→\n   167→**Result:** FAILED\n   168→- ✅ \"the creature\" still correctly merged with \"the monster\" and \"the wretch\" (maintained from Attempt 2)\n   169→- ❌ \"my father\" (53), \"Father\" (6), \"M.\" (23), \"M. Frankenstein\" (2), \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" (1) STILL separate\n   170→- ❌ \"Walton\" (6) and \"Captain Walton\" (1) STILL separate\n   171→- ❌ \"Clerval\" (55) and \"M. Clerval\" (2) STILL separate\n   172→- ❌ \"my mother\" (10) and \"Madame Frankenstein\" (1) STILL separate\n   173→\n   174→**Analysis:** The validation changes had ZERO IMPACT. This proves the problem is NOT in validation. Possible root causes:\n   175→1. Candidate pairs are not being generated (despite Attempt 2 claiming to fix this)\n   176→2. LLM pairwise merge is rejecting the pairs\n   177→3. Merging logic after validation is failing\n   178→4. The pairs are being generated and validated but then rejected in a later stage\n   179→\n   180→**Next Steps:** Need to add detailed logging at every stage to diagnose the root cause.\n   181→\n   182→### Attempt 4: Added comprehensive diagnostic logging to merge pipeline\n   183→**Issue:** Critical #1 from Attempt 3 - Need to trace exactly where the merge pipeline is failing for \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"\n   184→\n   185→**Root Cause Investigation:**\n   186→After Attempts 2 and 3, we know:\n   187→- Candidate pair generation was enhanced (Attempt 2)\n   188→- Validation logic was enhanced (Attempt 3)\n   189→- Yet NO merges are happening for fragmented characters\n   190→\n   191→This suggests one of three problems:\n   192→1. Candidate pairs are still not being generated properly\n   193→2. LLM is rejecting the pairs in pairwise merge decision\n   194→3. Some other stage in the pipeline is failing\n   195→\n   196→**Fix Applied:**\n   197→- Modified `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` lines 1051-1059: Added logging of all candidate pairs (first 50)\n   198→- Modified `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` lines 1069-1109: Enhanced logging for merge evaluation, validation, and acceptance/rejection\n   199→  - Added tracking for \"father\", \"frankenstein\", \"clerval\", \"walton\" pairs (in addition to existing wilson/gatsby tracking)\n   200→  - Log LLM decision (same_person, confidence)\n   201→  - Log validation result (is_valid, confidence)\n   202→  - Log merge acceptance/rejection with reasons\n   203→- Modified `src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py` lines 973-990: Added logging in relational/descriptive pairing section\n   204→  - Log top relational/descriptive names found\n   205→  - Log top proper names used for pairing\n   206→  - Flag critical pairs like \"my father\" + \"Alphonse Frankenstein\" with INFO level\n   207→\n   208→**Testing:** All 444 tests pass.\n   209→\n   210→**Expected Impact:** Logging will reveal exactly where the merge pipeline fails:\n   211→- If pairs aren't generated: We'll see missing pairs in candidate list\n   212→- If LLM rejects: We'll see same=False or low confidence in LLM decision logs\n   213→- If validation rejects: We'll see is_valid=False in validation logs\n   214→- If merge fails after validation: We'll see acceptance logs missing for valid pairs\n   215→\n   216→**Next Action:** Re-run analysis with enhanced logging to diagnose the root cause. The logs will guide the next fix.\n   217→\n   218→## Previous Text: gatsby\n   219→- **Result:** FAILED after 5 attempts (4.05/10)\n   220→- **Status:** Marked complete in manifest.json\n   221→\n   222→## Output Files (Attempt 3)\n   223→- HTML: output/frankenstein/report.html\n   224→- JSON: output/frankenstein/analysis.json\n   225→\n   226→## Pipeline Notes (Attempt 3)\n   227→- Analysis completed successfully in 69m 26s\n   228→- Pipeline metrics: 369 LLM calls, 861,167 tokens\n   229→- Structure: 25 chapters detected ✓\n   230→- Characters: 51 characters found (down from 49 in Attempt 2 - expected to decrease, actually increased)\n   231→- Character profiles: 26 profiles generated (up from 14 in Attempt 2)\n   232→- Pronunciations: 663 words flagged (same count as Attempt 2)\n   233→- Narrator detected: Victor Frankenstein (first-person) ✓\n   234→- Models used: qwen3-next:80b-a3b-instruct-q8_0 for character extraction, qwen3:30b-instruct for other tasks\n   235→- LLM errors: Several transient 500/EOF errors during character profiling (Elizabeth, Clerval, my father, Margaret) - resulted in 3 low-confidence profiles\n   236→- Warning: \"Narrator 'Vict